For those rasons and for other which I cannot express today for lack of time, Mr. Speaker, I appeal to my colleagues who received from the Canadian people a clear mandate to change the course of its history. I urge Hon. Members of the Opposition to join forces with us and help us finally to do justice to this nation which has been consistently demanding CBC cleanup. Are we going to betray their confidence and deny them something they have a right to demand? Essentially, Mr. Speaker, 1 am respectfully asking the House to say that the Government should introduce in the House of Commons legislation that would:
-dissolve the CBC, Radio Canada, Radio-Canada International, Entreprises Radio-Canada Enterprise, shut down their offices in New York, Washington, London, Paris, Moscow and wherever they may be, all their existing production centres, in other words the French-language T.V. and radio stations, the English-language T.V. and radio stations and all other components and/or affiliates;
-create a new corporation under a new name to be established by law;
-provide for the immediate termination of any mandate given to, and/or by any member of the Board, management, French-language services, English-language services, the Ottawa area, and any other existing council and committee, whether advisory, executive, audit, industrial relations or whatever;
-provide for mechanisms to immediately replace those people by other qualified people, with provisions for every other then employee of the CBC to immediately resume his or her respective job or even apply for any other then vacant job, according to his or her only qualifications and ability;
-immediately recognize all existing labour unions and all existing collective agreements.
And even before the Government decides to pass such legislation, we should require the CBC to be put under immediate guardianship. Thus the Government of Canada clearly will have the broadcasting Crown Corporation arrange within the shortest possible time for a distinctive, self-sufficient programming and a more Canadian content, and the implementation of the principles so dear to the hearts of Canadians, a vital tool for the cultural development of French speaking and English speaking Canadians, a true springboard for all the artists and workers of our two great Canadian cultures and other ethnic groups, an ethical framework that will finally present a solid moral character, a new sound spirit and new fair-play tendancies consistent with justice and merit, to finally achieve sound management of public monies.
Finally, Mr. Speaker, I am calling on all Hon. Members from both Opposition parties to even suggest and recommend to us in each province qualified Canadians who, above political
Broadcasting
affiliations, can work together to reach for the goals that Canadians have long since set for CBC officers. Almost on the eve of the Easter holidays, Mr. Speaker, let us rid ourselves of evil and celebrate a new era for our national broadcasting that will fulfill the true expectations of the Canadian people!
